"where": "スライド3",
"en": "The Stack",
"ja": ""
},
{
"i": 12,
"where": "スライド3",
"en": "A stack is a Last-In-First-Out (LIFO) data structure where data is \"pushed\" on to the top of the stack and \"popped\" off the top",
"ja": ""
},
{
"i": 16,
"where": "スライド3",
"en": "The stack is a conceptual area of main memory (RAM) which is designated by the OS when a program is started",
"ja": ""
},
{
"i": 17,
"where": "スライド3",
"en": "Different OSes start it at different addresses by their own convention, or if they’re using address space layout randomization (ASLR)",
"ja": ""
},
{
"i": 18,
"where": "スライド3",
"en": "Book page 107",
"ja": ""
},
{
"i": 19,
"where": "スライド4",
"en": "Process Memory",
"ja": ""
},
{
"i": 20,
"where": "スライド4",
"en": "The Stack 2",
"ja": ""
},
{
"i": 21,
"where": "スライド4",
"en": "By convention the stack grows toward lower memory addresses. Adding something to the stack means the top of the stack is now at a lower memory address",
"ja": ""
},
